#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0061dcd5-ae6d-5db2-aa07-fe63f3a74741" :question/math-academy-id "e-15612" :question/problem "Find a general expression describing all of the zeros of $y = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of\n\n$y=\\sin X$.\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n$X =n\\pi$\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= nπ \\\\\n\\frac{x}{2} &= nπ \\\\\nx &= 2nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x =2n\\pi$, where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "67598690-76eb-5478-9497-69ef0aa50e15" :question/math-academy-id "e-15613" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =\\sin \\left(3x + 45^{\\circ} \\right)$. Find an expression that gives the $x$-values at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = 3x + 45^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sin X\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX = -90^\\circ+360^\\circ\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= -90^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n3x + 45^{∘} &= -90^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n3x &= -45^{∘} - 90^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n3x &= -135^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\nx &= -45^{∘} + 120^{∘} \\cdot n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=-45^\\circ + 120^\\circ \\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "59ce8792-ebf0-5340-b005-1655827c7c40" :question/math-academy-id "e-15614" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 5\\cos \\left(2x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)$. Find an expression that gives the $x$-values at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value. Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX =2x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos X\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$X = 2n\\pi$\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 2nπ \\\\\n2x + \\frac{π}{3} &= 2nπ \\\\\n2x &= -\\frac{π}{3} + 2nπ \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{π}{6} + nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96bd349f-3c1b-5a39-94f2-5f93257c0858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 10^{∘} + 72^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201223} {:answer/id #uuid "aa23552c-028f-54a3-a4f1-fbdc172edbee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 300^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201224} {:answer/id #uuid "292efc7f-20ab-50ad-b48b-7f45e1f016d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-50^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201225} {:answer/id #uuid "3adf127c-9897-5ac7-9aa2-255b35e658c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-10^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201226} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2c1d7f-2ba3-5152-a57e-58e29e167b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 50^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96bd349f-3c1b-5a39-94f2-5f93257c0858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 10^{∘} + 72^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201223} :answer-field/id #uuid "027de7cd-c6e3-584d-92c9-854a635c48c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201222}] :question/id #uuid "81a5af91-f24b-58ee-ac26-21e2520ec16f" :question/math-academy-id "q-236968" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 6\\sin (5x + 40^{∘})$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943ff65c-9441-5f07-b9c7-abb58d053589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 45^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203472} {:answer/id #uuid "b81b47fe-fd69-5a9b-bffd-5a8eb39808be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203473} {:answer/id #uuid "6af4f485-4cd4-5102-8c91-1a4944eb8142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-45^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203474} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5f7b83-3296-53b5-86dc-ffbd52431e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 90^{∘} + n \\cdot 360^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203475} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6976b7-5ba2-5883-89ae-ecc22a193065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "943ff65c-9441-5f07-b9c7-abb58d053589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 45^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203472} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b4f1fe4-ae9d-57ac-bdfc-336c8bbe1583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203471}] :question/id #uuid "6cc77154-ecae-5ece-8bcc-9eb3f418b440" :question/math-academy-id "q-29132" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y = 3\\cos (x + 45^{∘})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."}]}
